part of the issue with things like this  is people defining value for people that  aren't them  I am Nicolas be with reason TV and today  we're talking with Courtney baliker  she's the director of a forthcoming  movie called little pink house which is  about the kylo eminent domain decision  from a few years back Courtney thanks  for talking to us thanks for having me  recap the story of Suzette kylo and her  little pink calves Suzette kylo was a  blue-collar working-class woman studying  to be a nurse in 1999 found a house that  was broken down she was just coming out  of a marriage all five boys had grown  gone out of the house and she really  just wanted a peaceful quiet life and  she found this dilapidated home built it  back up with her bare hands and the  thing that was remarkable about it had a  beautiful view of the Thames River in  New London Connecticut and so she built  it up put furniture in it and painted it  pink meanwhile Pfizer pharmaceutical was  on the other side of the Thames River  and they had just invented a drug called  viagra and they were looking to expand  their facility and the city wanted to  make sure that they would be able to do  that into New London but they needed  space to build a campus hotel things for  Pfizer pharmaceutical so the governor of  Connecticut at the time Governor John G  Rowland made a very friendly environment  in New London so that Pfizer would be  able to acquire the land and use it for  their space unfortunately Sue's Aquila's  property was on one of the parcels of  that land and they used they meaning the  city used eminent domain to make sure  that she and her neighbors would be gone  so they kind of like they declare the  whole area blighted or whatever and then  they say we're taking it through the  power of eminent domain yeah and then  they just immediately were turning it  over to a private developer it was it  was a little bit of a process but the  word blight was used because in order to  use eminent domain you have to show well  this area needs to be redeveloped maybe  it's dangerous it was not blight these  were older Victorian homes some of the  people that lived in these homes were  born in them most of the neighborhood  were elderly people  and the city basically offered them  money is that that's always how it  starts but of course it they low ball  and they low ball but the real issue is  you can't define someone's value you  can't I can't go up to you and say look  I know what the market value of your  house is I'll give that to you well that  that value could be way more than money  maybe your grandfather built that house  maybe this is your dream house that  you've always wanted what's the price  tag on that so part of the issue with  things like this is people defining  value for people that aren't them now  the the case ended up going to the  Supreme Court  yes and Susette Kelo did not win that  case she didn't she said kilo fought all  the way and she did not want to be the  face of eminent domain in this country  she wanted a quiet peaceful existence  but she knew that what was happening was  wrong and so she stood up and it went to  the state Supreme Court first and she  actually did win on that but they split  the baby as they said some of her  neighbors would have to leave while she  and a few others could stay and so she  and a few others said no either we all  stay or we all go and then when they  revisited that decision they lost and  then it went up to the United States  Supreme Court where she lost again so  this is a classic David and Goliath  story or I don't know what the female  version of David is but it's Dolores  versus Goliath and what's interesting in  Hollywood is that a lot of the times  it's you know it's it's an agent of the  government you know a social worker or a  teacher or a lawyer going against an  evil corporation so this kind of flips  that a little bit doesn't it does flip  it a little bit the agent in this case  was the New London Development Committee  and they were basically an organization  that was put in place by the government  really to bring in a corporation like  Pfizer so that they could redevelop it  it is a David and Goliath story because  all of the powerful people all the  people with money and all of the people  with connections and pull were in the  driver's seat and Suzette kilo didn't  have  Pyles money wasn't lawyered up in the  beginning of this process and so it  really was a grassroots David and  Goliath story when you look at Suzette  kilo and what she did essentially what  happened is eminent domain was not used  in its proper way eminent domain is  typically used to expand roads build  hospitals build schools it's not used so  that I can take from one private  individual and give it to another  private individual which is essentially  what happened but there are certain  Supreme Court justices who said yes but  it was for the betterment of the  community because it would create more  tax revenue and and that also it went  across ideological lines there were some  conservatives and liberals in making the  decision that yet the government can  take whatever property they want as long  as it's you know good for society or  something when the decision came out in  2005 it was a very very close decision  it was a four or five decision and it  was remarkable how it United the country  no matter what your politics were that  90% of Americans agreed that it was a  bad decision I mean in a weird way I  mean they won she lost in the Supreme  Court of the United States but then it  touched off this kind of widespread  rebellion against them and it domain  abuse yeah when I talked to some people  about is that such a bummer ending she  didn't win well that's true but  unfortunately I think very tragic things  have to happen sometimes before people  will pay attention and I think that the  silver lining behind this very bad  decision this very awful thing that she  went through really ten-year ordeal that  she and her neighbors went through  wondering if they were going to be  homeless being asked to pay rent on  their own property at one point this was  not a quick thing but the silver lining  is that it was such an outrageous  decision that everyone was talking about  it major media outlets were covering it  and most people agree that it was a very  bad thing and I don't think people would  be talking about eminent domain abuse  had she won and as an epilogue about 45  states curbed their eminent domain laws  so that this type of thing couldn't  happen again  and tell us what ended up being built on  the land there are some feral cats that  are building little I guess  dirt beds and rats and weeds so it's it  really turned out to be a great project  Pfizer ended up not building nothing was  built in fact 10 years have gone by has  gone by now and there have been ideas  that percolate one of the funniest  things I read last year was that  somebody had the idea of you know what  we should build and this parcel of land  we should maybe build like a parking lot  with like little houses next to it that  people could and the writer said you  mean like the one that you tore down the  little pink house you start filming in  the fall yes so we have we are fully  funded we are greenlit film our  production schedule is to shoot in  September and we're in the casting  process now and we are just so excited  to get this movie made and out into the  world we want to entertain people we  want to tell us through that story but  we also want to tell people what  happened eminent domain abuse is still a  problem there are little pink houses all  across the country and so there's a  social action campaign behind the film -  and we really want to put an end to what  happened to Suzette well thank you so  much we'll look forward when it comes  out  Courtney baliker the movie is little  pink house it's about Suzette kilo  fighting the government and 